---
import Exhibit from "../components/Exhibit.astro";
import ChapterOpener from "../components/ChapterOpener.astro";
import Term from "../components/Term.astro";
import PractitionerNotes from "../components/PractitionerNotes.astro";
import Definitions from "../components/Definitions.astro";
import Pager from "../components/Pager.astro";
import ConformalPlayground from "../islands/ConformalPlayground.svelte";
import { load, pct, type Frame } from "../lib/data";
import { pack } from "../lib/pack";

const meta = load("meta");
const metrics = load("metrics");
const golden = load("golden");
const cal = load<Frame>("calibration");
const vol = load<Frame>("vol");
const sm = metrics.vol.split_absolute;
const calYears = `${meta.splits.cal.start.slice(0, 4)}–${meta.splits.cal.end.slice(0, 4)}`;
const firstYear = meta.splits.test.start.slice(0, 4);
const level = 1 - meta.alpha;
const nCal: number = meta.splits.cal.rows;
const k = Math.ceil((nCal + 1) * level - 1e-9);
---
<section class="chapter" id="intervals" aria-labelledby="intervals-h">
  <ChapterOpener n="4" title="Honest intervals" minutes={3} question="How do you turn a guess into a range you can trust?" hid="intervals-h" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Here is the idea behind <Term id="conformal-split" title="Split conformal prediction: set the range from the forecast's errors on a held-out period.">split conformal prediction</Term>,
      in three steps. Run the forecast over a period it was not trained on, here {calYears}, and
      write down how far off it was each day. Sort those {nCal} misses from smallest to largest.
      To promise {pct(level, 0)}, take the {k}th smallest and put a range that wide around every future
      forecast.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the future behaves like the calibration years, the range will hold on at least {pct(level, 0)}
      of days. That guarantee is mathematical, not a modelling assumption, and it needs nothing about
      the shape of returns. Try it: move the promise and watch the cut-off and the test result change.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="exhibit-wide">
    <Exhibit
      letter="E"
      title="Set the cut-off from past misses, then check it on the future"
      source={`Absolute errors of the log-volatility forecast on ${nCal} calibration days (${calYears}); the cut-off is applied to ${meta.splits.test.rows.toLocaleString("en-GB")} test days. Computed in your browser; the code reproduces the Python reference values exactly. The promise you set here also drives Exhibit F.`}
      id="exhibit-e"
    >
      <ConformalPlayground
        client:visible
        data={pack(cal, vol)}
        calYears={calYears}
      />
      <table slot="table">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">At the {pct(level, 0)} promise</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Year, at the {pct(level, 0)} promise</th><th scope="col">Held</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {Object.entries(sm.by_year).map(([yr, v]) => <tr><td>{yr}</td><td>{pct(v as number)}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Exhibit>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      At {pct(level, 0)} the textbook method held on {pct(sm.coverage)} of test days: close, but short.
      The year strip shows where it went wrong. In {firstYear} it held on only {pct(sm.by_year[firstYear])}
      of days. The calibration years were calm; {firstYear} was not. The guarantee assumes the future is
      exchangeable with the past, and a pandemic broke that assumption.
    </p>
  </div>

  <PractitionerNotes>
    <p>
      Score S = |y − μ̂| on log volatility; q̂ is the ⌈(n+1)(1−α)⌉-th smallest calibration score, giving
      [μ̂ − q̂, μ̂ + q̂], i.e. a multiplicative range on volatility. At α = {meta.alpha}, q̂ =
      {" "}{golden.levels[String(meta.alpha)].split_q.toFixed(4)}. Coverage is guaranteed marginally under
      exchangeability, which overlapping 5-day targets and regime shifts both violate. Normalized
      scores and conformalized quantile regression were also tested: {pct(metrics.vol.split_normalized.coverage)} and
      {" "}{pct(metrics.vol.cqr.coverage)} overall.
    </p>
  </PractitionerNotes>

  <Definitions
    items={[
      { id: "conformal-split", term: "Split conformal", def: "Set the range from the forecast's sorted errors on a period it was not trained on." },
      { id: "exchangeable", term: "Exchangeable", def: "The order of the days does not matter: tomorrow looks like a random draw from the past." },
    ]}
  />
  <Pager id="intervals" />
</section>
